WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.780
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.400
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

9
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:24.700
Hello, everyone.

10
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.020
Good morning, our afternoon, our nighttime.

11
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.300
I like our intro.

12
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.740
This is our new intro.

13
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.060
Today, we are here to talk about mafia.

14
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.940
Season 6, Episode 6.

15
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:45.000
And, Ange, you and I were kind of texting back and forth about some fun, what would you

16
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.680
call them, asides, mini deep dives that we went on as a result of this episode.

17
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.560
And you have one that I was like, don't tell me anymore.

18
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.260
Don't tell me anymore.

19
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.700
I want to hear it on the podcaSt.

20
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.320
I can't wait.

21
00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.980
I have one that I just dipped my toe in, you guys.

22
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.440
There's so much more to it.

23
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.960
I actually bought a book about it.

24
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.820
Your own deep dive led you to now buy a book?

25
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:08.620
Yeah, exactly.

26
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.220
That's what happened with Clementine Paddleford.

27
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.520
I have her book.

28
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:13.780
We became obsessed.

29
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:14.540
I know.

30
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:15.260
Oh, my.

31
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.840
How about you hit us with a summary and we'll get into it?

32
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.760
Well, I'm going to tell you that this was written by Brent Forrester and directed by Dave

33
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.560
Rogers, and my summary is this.

34
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.840
Michael is visited by an insurance salesman whom Dwight and Andy convince him is a member

35
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.160
of the Italian mafia.

36
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.920
Michael then feels pressured to purchase a very expensive insurance package that he does

37
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:39.560
not need.

38
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:45.380
Meanwhile, Kevin takes over Jim's office because Jim and Pam are on their honeymoon.

39
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.000
In Puerto Rico.

40
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.060
Yes.

41
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.380
But everyone keeps calling them.

42
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.280
We'll get to it.

43
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.680
Yes, they do.

44
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.600
And Kevin accidentally cancels their credit cards.

45
00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:58.660
Amazing.

46
00:01:59.560 --> 00:01:59.820
All right.

47
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.140
Fast fact number one.

48
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.760
I reached out to Brent Forrester.

49
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.580
We always love to hear from Brent.

50
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.160
Oh, Brent is so fun.

51
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.940
Like I said, he wrote this episode and he was very interested in discussing the tone of

52
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.600
this episode, which he pointed out is a little different.

53
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.500
Here's what he had to say.

54
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.560
I wrote the office episode called Mafia, considered now a minor comedy classic, but it was controversial

55
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.380
at the time of shooting.

56
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.100
Why?

57
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.680
Because what makes the office great is this tone of naturalism, realism, groundedness.

58
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:38.340
And this episode pushed in the opposite direction tonally.

59
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.220
The whole premise is farcical.

60
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.820
Michael Scott believes an Italian-American insurance agent is a mafioso.

61
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.320
That's farce.

62
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:52.300
Then we added Ed Helms dressed as a mechanic in order to justify bringing a wrench into a

63
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:52.600
restaurant.

64
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:59.180
Things got tense on set between the guys behind the camera, the director Dave Rogers, and

65
00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:05.380
me, and the guys in front of the camera, Steve Carell, Rainn Wilson, and Ed Helms, who had

66
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.580
to play this material as real.

67
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.800
Very, very challenging.

68
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.920
So I remember this tension.

69
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.040
The guys in general did not love when we pushed into farce.

70
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.300
There was always a lot of pushback from all of us in the caSt.

71
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.220
We just loved that early season realism more than anything.

72
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.400
Well, you know, Jenna, I know some of the bigger storylines, and particularly with Dwight,

73
00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.440
would crack us up, right?

74
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.040
Like, we could get on board with some of the big storylines.

75
00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.340
But when the whole entire storyline was so absurd, that's when it was harder, I think,

76
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:42.780
for us.

77
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.580
Because we needed the balance of, like, the smaller, like, just, like, run-of-the-mill

78
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.600
daily work life mixed in with something crazy.

79
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.040
But this was kind of just crazy start to finish.

80
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.580
It's funny, because in this rewatch, I have loved these farcical storylines.

81
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.360
I mean, remember when those guys all put on fake mustaches and they went to Utica to steal

82
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.780
the industrial copier?

83
00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.440
Yes, I was cracking up.

84
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.680
I mean, I also feel like if you go all the way back to the injury and Michael grilling

85
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.380
his foot on a George Foreman grill, I mean, that's, I don't know if that's necessarily

86
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.640
farce, but that's definitely very broad.

87
00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.460
Yeah, I just think the difference for this one is it was the whole entire storyline.

88
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:26.780
Oh, your cat is here.

89
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:27.700
Hi, Sunny Cat.

90
00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.360
Sunny has just walked into the zoom.

91
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.200
Wait, I wish I could take a picture.

92
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.100
I wish I could take a picture of this.

93
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.080
This is so cute.

94
00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.840
Jenna's cat is, like, the whole screen.

95
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:36.880
How are you?

96
00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:37.780
How are you?

97
00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:38.960
On the microphone.

98
00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.040
He's just come for a visit.

99
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.080
What is so funny is I just saw his tail go through your plant, round the corner, and

100
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:47.840
then there he was.

101
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:48.540
So cute.

102
00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:55.000
This is our life right now, you guys, with, like, the minimum occupancy in studio is I'm

103
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.040
here and Jenna's in her closet.

104
00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.660
With my cat.

105
00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.360
With her cat.

106
00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.360
He sits in my office most of the day with me, so he wanted to say hi.

107
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.860
But yeah, I mean, you're right.

108
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.480
It was more of a side storyline.

109
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.680
But the injury was, like, that was, you know, he's asking Pam to rub butter on his foot.

110
00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:15.940
I don't know.

111
00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.620
I have to say, in the rewatch, I love these storylines.

112
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:24.260
And by the way, I'm also noticing that it's coming on the heels of what Jen Salata was

113
00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:30.380
saying last week about how her storyline in the promotion was so dramatic.

114
00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:38.700
And it really goes to show, like, how much influence an individual writer and their script can have on the tone of an episode.

115
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.420
Oh, 100%.

116
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.300
And also, the characters they write for.

117
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.280
Yes.

118
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:45.380
All right.

119
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:53.000
Well, speaking of storylines, my fast fact number two is all about a deleted storyline in this episode.

120
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:59.480
Which, by the way, this deleted storyline would still show up in the episode description on Netflix.

121
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.480
It's still there on iTunes as well.

122
00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:02.320
You're kidding.

123
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.980
Because it's not in it at all.

124
00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.420
Not in it at all.

125
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.560
I was surprised, Jenna, when I watched the deleted scenes.

126
00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.600
It's the whole entire runner of the deleted scenes.

127
00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.060
It was a big storyline.

128
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.740
Let me tell you what it is.

129
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.220
Because, oh, I had a reaction.

130
00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.440
Yeah, you did.

131
00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:19.800
Sure.

132
00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.920
Erin was supposed to be cleaning Pam's watercolor.

133
00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:30.300
Her famous watercolor that Michael bought from her art show.

134
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.920
She sprays the glass with this cleaner.

135
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.800
And then Creed kind of distracts her by chatting her up.

136
00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.480
And the spray seeps under the glass.

137
00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.680
And the watercolor is ruined.

138
00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:43.940
Yeah.

139
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.320
Erin destroys Pam's watercolor while she's on her honeymoon, was the storyline.

140
00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.680
If you watch the deleted scenes, you watch it melt.

141
00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:59.200
Like, all the colors, like, slide down to the bottom of the frame.

142
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.480
And I literally went, like, I forgot it was in the story at one point.

143
00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.740
And then there's another deleted scene where she asks Kelly to help her fix it.

144
00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.680
And Kelly's like, oh, yeah, I can do watercolor.

145
00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.040
But what Kelly does is she, like, adds a rainbow and a sunshine.

146
00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.660
But the painting is still totally melty.

147
00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:22.320
And then in the end, Erin calls Pam on her honeymoon and says she ruined it.

148
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.080
And Pam has this line where she says, you know what?

149
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.680
I will be mad at you about ruining my painting when I get back.

150
00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.040
Stop calling me.

151
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.400
Tell people to stop calling us.

152
00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:33.140
Mm-hmm.

153
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.300
There was a scene that let up before she ruined your painting.

154
00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:38.700
I'm going to save it.

155
00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.180
I'm going to share it when it would have happened in the episode, okay?

156
00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:41.820
Okay.

157
00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:45.520
That sort of set up why she even started cleaning it in the first place.

158
00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:46.880
Oh, okay.

159
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:47.620
Yeah.

160
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:51.060
But I think it will make more sense if I bring it up where it would have occurred.

161
00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:52.180
I like it.

162
00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.760
After we read the script in our table read, which is the week before we're meant to shoot it,

163
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:05.660
I, like, as soon as I started reading it, I got hot inside, you know?

164
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.800
Like, you heat up, and I could barely focus on the rest of the table read.

165
00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.420
You had a physical reaction.

166
00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:12.960
I did.

167
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.180
You, like, flushed.

168
00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.280
Like, your cheeks were red.

169
00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:15.800
Yes!

170
00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.860
I waited until after the table read.

171
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:19.800
Everybody left.

172
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:20.880
And this was unusual.

173
00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.740
I did not normally stay after a table read and, like, give my feelings.

174
00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:27.100
No, no.

175
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.880
No, in fact, we usually had to scooch out because we had to go to hair and makeup to get back to set.

176
00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.660
Yeah, to go back to shoot.

177
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:36.720
But I was like, listen, you cannot destroy Pam's painting.

178
00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:37.700
You can't do it.

179
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.500
It's the heartbeat of the show.

180
00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:40.840
It's on the wall.

181
00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.740
It's her relationship with Michael.

182
00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:44.260
It's hope.

183
00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:45.600
It's so many things.

184
00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.440
Like, you don't understand.

185
00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:48.380
Like, you can't make this a gag.

186
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.460
And Paul Lieberstein was like, I think it's funny.

187
00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.780
What are you talking about?

188
00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:55.700
And it was a battle.

189
00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:56.700
I was like, no, no, no.

190
00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.020
You don't understand.

191
00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.840
Like, you cannot do it.

192
00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.180
And I called Greg.

193
00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.320
I, like, made my case to anyone who would listen.

194
00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:07.000
They shot it anyway.

195
00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:07.980
They were like, I'm sorry.

196
00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:08.840
It's staying in.

197
00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:10.080
We really like this gag.

198
00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.240
We think it's great.

199
00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:12.800
We like it.

200
00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:13.440
Oh, they were so wrong.

201
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:14.700
They were so wrong.

202
00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.960
Lady, after we shot it, I went into the edit bay.

203
00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.080
And I was like, you have to take this out.

204
00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.280
You have to take this out.

205
00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.260
I appealed to Dave Rogers.

206
00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:26.660
I appealed to Brent Forrester.

207
00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.460
Like, I would not shut up about it.

208
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.140
Finally, in the end, they took it out.

209
00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:33.660
They took it out.

210
00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:34.580
And I was so relieved.

211
00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:39.880
But you'll see next week, there's not Pam's watercolor on the wall.

212
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.180
Because that was the week they were editing and still deciding.

213
00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.740
So if you look, Pam's watercolor is missing for a couple of episodes.

214
00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:52.400
And we just sneakily put it back on the wall without saying anything.

215
00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:53.860
Oh, my gosh.

216
00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:58.240
So for those episodes, there's no painting because you were still pleading your case.

217
00:09:58.640 --> 00:09:59.060
Yes.

218
00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.980
But I would not give up.

219
00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.540
And then, I mean, listen.

220
00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:08.260
And the whole series ends on Pam's watercolor.

221
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:09.100
I know.

222
00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.580
And I couldn't imagine it ending any other way.

223
00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.660
I mean, that made me bawl my eyes out.

224
00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:14.540
I know.

225
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:20.900
And you better believe that I marched right up to Paul Lieberstein when I read that ending

226
00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:21.600
of the finale.

227
00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:27.500
And I was like, Paul, do you see now why Aaron couldn't destroy the watercolor?

228
00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:28.720
Do you see?

229
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.160
You held on to that for years.

230
00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:31.820
Yes.

231
00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:39.400
Well, I'm so glad you did because it represents such a tender moment between Michael and Pam.

232
00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.800
And it is the hope.

233
00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:41.860
It's hope.

234
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.260
It's hope in a frame.

235
00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.420
It's hope in a frame.

236
00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.080
I wish I'd said that.

237
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.600
It's hope in a frame.

238
00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.280
You're welcome.

239
00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:51.740
Oh, man.

240
00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.700
Well, I'm really glad you fought hard for it.

241
00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.940
I knew you didn't want it to be destroyed.

242
00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:57.100
I remembered all of that.

243
00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.260
I did not remember how hard you had to fight for it.

244
00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:01.140
Yeah.

245
00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:05.520
I mean, there were two things in the series that I fought hard for.

246
00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:10.620
I would consistently fight that there be no infidelity in Jim and Pam's relationship.

247
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.840
That would often get brought up.

248
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.920
And John and I would go say, no, no, no, no, no, no.

249
00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.620
And this was the other thing.

250
00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:19.940
Don't destroy the painting.

251
00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:20.740
Yeah.

252
00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:25.740
Well, Jenna, the amazing Randy Cordray shared with us that the whole destroying of Pam's

253
00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.640
painting was considered a mini-stunt.

254
00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:29.680
Oh.

255
00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.380
Here's how they had to do it.

256
00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:36.220
Our special effects coordinator, Mike Thompson, created what the special effects folks call

257
00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.940
an A-B reaction.

258
00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:43.820
It's where two different substances are inert and non-reactive until mixed together.

259
00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.260
Oh, I remember that from chemistry class.

260
00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.740
I was going to say, you didn't know we were going to have a little bit of science today.

261
00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:52.740
The A substance was pre-applied over the watercolor.

262
00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.540
It dried clear and was not visible.

263
00:11:55.940 --> 00:12:02.140
The B substance, a reactive, also clear liquid, was what Aaron sprayed.

264
00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:08.800
There were minute openings in the top of the frame to allow the B liquid to run inside

265
00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:09.600
the glass.

266
00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:15.000
And when the B chemical reacted with the A, it changed the colors and achieved the desired

267
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.740
result of the gag, which was the watercolor's melting.

268
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.460
He even sent us a video of their practice run, which we can put in stories.

269
00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.620
Oh, then so people can see the melting of the watercolor.

270
00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:28.340
Yes.

271
00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:29.180
Wow.

272
00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:30.700
But I thought that was so interesting.

273
00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:31.940
Look what all they had to do.

274
00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:36.680
To make this moment happen, I mean, ultimately, I'm glad it didn't make it in the show, right?

275
00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:39.920
But pretty cool to see what all they had to do.

276
00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:46.260
Gosh, it's sort of like a very mini version of everything Randy was doing to get a horse

277
00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.660
to go over Niagara Falls.

278
00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:48.580
Oh, no.

279
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.180
You know, like, how do I make this happen?

280
00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.640
And then, oh, we actually cut it out of the episode.

281
00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:53.960
Right.

282
00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:55.880
All right.

283
