Oh, I see how it is.

358
00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:14.450
All right.

359
00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:17.590
Why don't we take a break, and then we'll come back and break down this episode.

360
00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:18.390
Awesome.

361
00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:29.600
Well, we are back.

362
00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:35.340
We're in the conference room because Michael is discussing the fundamentals of business.

363
00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:36.380
Yeah.

364
00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:42.960
He has written on a dry erase board, handshakes, small talk, shut up and sell.

365
00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:43.780
Yeah.

366
00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:48.000
He invites people to give suggestions for small talk.

367
00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.320
He really likes Meredith's idea, and he says, come on up here.

368
00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:52.280
She says, the weekend.

369
00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:53.860
He's like, that's great.

370
00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:54.800
Let's riff on that.

371
00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:55.840
Get on up here, Meredith.

372
00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:56.900
That's right.

373
00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:57.960
They start to role play.

374
00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.120
Michael's like, so, Meredith, how was your weekend?

375
00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:02.160
What did you do?

376
00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:07.700
And Meredith says, well, I caught my son taking a dump on the upper part of the toilet.

377
00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:09.760
He calls it an upper decker.

378
00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.480
And Michael's like, oh, God, okay, you know, just go sit down.

379
00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:14.960
That made me laugh out loud.

380
00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:16.620
It made me laugh, too.

381
00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:22.320
And I remember when we were filming this, we all cracked up, and I went and looked in

382
00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:23.000
the shooting draft.

383
00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.380
That line is not in there.

384
00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:25.820
The upper decker line?

385
00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:27.700
It's not in the shooting draft.

386
00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:30.140
This is what was in the shooting draft.

387
00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:33.860
Meredith says, I caught my son doing my fur coat.

388
00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.260
I had to shave off one of the sleeves.

389
00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:37.280
Oh.

390
00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.420
And Michael's like, gross.

391
00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:41.640
So I texted Kate.

392
00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.240
I said, Kate, did you improvise the upper decker line?

393
00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:46.520
I mean, come on.

394
00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:47.520
Where did that come from?

395
00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.620
And she said, no.

396
00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:55.020
Paul Lieberstein whispered it in my ear after the very first take.

397
00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:56.440
Oh, my gosh.

398
00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:01.220
That just goes to show you that when Paul Lieberstein was in the background of a scene,

399
00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:03.760
he was still writing the show in his head.

400
00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:04.360
Right.

401
00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:05.840
He was pitching jokes.

402
00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.680
And the rest of us had no idea what he whispered in her ear.

403
00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:11.780
So when she said it, we all heard it for the first time.

404
00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:13.380
Oh, brilliant.

405
00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:15.940
They would do that to us, you guys.

406
00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:21.460
And it would send us all into fits of giggles because we were ready and prepared for the

407
00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:23.440
crazy line about the fur coat, right?

408
00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:23.740
Right.

409
00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:26.500
And then all of a sudden, we get hit with the upper decker.

410
00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:33.380
I want to point out that at 49 seconds, you'll get a real good shot of the fact that Kate

411
00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:35.880
is wearing that Nanette Lepore vest again.

412
00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.040
This is the third time now.

413
00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:39.820
Jenna, I noticed that too.

414
00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:41.880
The woman loves that sweater veSt.

415
00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.000
It's a very expensive sweater veSt.

416
00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:52.320
I'm telling you, I was working with a stylist at the time, and it was like $200 back then.

417
00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:55.140
How does Meredith afford this vest?

418
00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:59.160
I don't know, but she wears it a lot, so she's getting her money out of it.

419
00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:00.180
She is.

420
00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.200
Well, the role play doesn't work.

421
00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:03.600
And Michael's so frustrated.

422
00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:07.520
He's like, God, I could fill a book with the stuff you guys don't know about business.

423
00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.760
And Ryan's like, go write it.

424
00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.000
Write a book.

425
00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:12.180
Yeah.

426
00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:14.720
So Michael immediately goes into his office.

427
00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:18.860
He starts dictating his book, The Fundamentals of Business by Michael Scott,

428
00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.700
one billion sold, more than the Bible.

429
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:23.220
Chapter one, The Businessman.

430
00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.220
And then he stops talking.

431
00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:25.840
Yeah.

432
00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:28.580
I got curious, as happens.

433
00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:33.220
I was like, how many copies of the Bible have been sold?

434
00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:34.740
I would think a lot.

435
00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:35.700
Yeah.

436
00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:36.820
Well, I'll tell you what.

437
00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:42.480
But it's so many that even if Michael's book, The Fundamentals of Business by Michael Scott,

438
00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.580
sold one billion copies, it would not be more than the Bible.

439
00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:53.440
The Guinness Book of World Records estimates that more than five billion copies of the Bible have

440
00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:54.040
been sold.

441
00:19:54.320 --> 00:20:00.600
The Quran has sold approximately 800 million copies, and the Book of Mormon has sold around

442
00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.120
120 million copies.

443
00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:07.740
They used, I guess, historical records and a variety of other sources to get those numbers.

444
00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:10.040
But I couldn't stop there, Angela.

445
00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:11.860
I had to know more.

446
00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:13.540
What else is there to know?

447
00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:19.080
So, the top-selling books in general are religious texts.

448
00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:24.820
But I needed to know, what is just the top-selling fiction book?

449
00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:25.680
Okay.

450
00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:27.480
What is it?

451
00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:32.180
It is Don Quixote by Cervantes.

452
00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.480
And it was published in 1605.

453
00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:40.520
It has sold approximately 500 million copies, and it has been translated into 145 languages.

454
00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:44.300
I had to read Don Quixote, by the way, in my college Spanish class.

455
00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:45.020
Oh.

456
00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:48.540
Well, you are one of the 500 million copies sold.

457
00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:49.360
I am.

458
00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.460
But you know what else is up there, lady?

459
00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:52.560
What?

460
00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:54.780
Another one you've contributed to.

461
00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:55.840
What?

462
00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:57.340
Lord of the Rings.

463
00:20:57.740 --> 00:20:58.180
Oh!

464
00:20:58.740 --> 00:20:59.320
Yeah.

465
00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:00.640
I believe it.

466
00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:01.180
Yeah.

467
00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:02.540
Mm-hmm.

468
00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:05.340
People love stories about a journey.

469
00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:07.260
I guess they do.

470
00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:08.300
It's true.

471
00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:11.220
Well, I have something to share about this scene.

472
00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:12.820
What is it?

473
00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:19.180
I was very curious what was in the shooting draft about Michael's book, if there was more.

474
00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:19.660
Oh.

475
00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:23.920
Because he just pauses and looks off into the distance, right?

476
00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:25.340
And then it cuts away.

477
00:21:25.460 --> 00:21:26.600
And I was like, was there more?

478
00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.920
Here's how this moment read in the shooting draft.

479
00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:33.740
Michael, you know, is talking into the recorder.

480
00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:34.300
Yeah.

481
00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.740
He says, The Fundamentals of Business by Michael Scott.

482
00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:39.960
There is a long beat.

483
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:42.240
Chapter one.

484
00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:47.600
The murderer walked into the garden worriedly, not happily.

485
00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:50.200
It was the happiest day of his life.

486
00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:51.900
He was going to space.

487
00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:52.980
What?

488
00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:54.640
Long beat.

489
00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:56.720
Chapter two.

490
00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:02.360
We laugh.

491
00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:04.400
I'm sure they did a whole bunch of alts, right?

492
00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.680
I bet there was a bunch of candy bags for this one.

493
00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:08.760
But that's what was in the shooting draft.

494
00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:12.000
Well, now we're going to get into the meat of this episode.

495
00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:18.120
Michael arrives to work, and there's a man sitting on the reception couch, waiting to

496
00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:18.700
see him.

497
00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:20.780
Aaron has allowed this.

498
00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:22.040
Michael's not pleased.

499
00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:23.520
Michael is so bombed.

500
00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.520
He's like, you have to protect my creative space.

501
00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:27.420
Yeah.

502
00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:31.480
I mean, she was like, well, I thought this could be part of your creative space.

503
00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:35.240
And he's like, just push creative space to the afternoon.

504
00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:36.980
What did I have?

505
00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:38.340
And she's like, free play.

506
00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:40.080
He's like, push free play to tomorrow.

507
00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.160
What is his schedule?

508
00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:43.060
I don't know.

509
00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:48.060
Well, this insurance salesman, Mr. Grotty, begins a presentation.

510
00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:49.240
Michael's so bored.

511
00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:53.180
He has a talking head where he says there's nothing more insulting than listening to a bad

512
00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:57.700
salesman, is like a good basketball player listening to a bad basketball player.

513
00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:05.320
And right after this talking head would have been a pretty important deleted scene for the

514
00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:07.280
Pam watercolor fiasco.

515
